I have been reading this exchange, and I found it pretty obvious what
you meant (unless I'm horribly mistaken).  The protection racket is a
limit on the player's available actions that arguably makes the game
harder.  If it makes the game harder, as you claim, then the comparison
makes sense.  And it's a good bit of wit.  So if you wanted that backed
up, you have it.

This does bring up the question of whether the user has to know if a
conduct is harder to really be trying a conduct.  Look at this
illustration.  Suppose I, in ignorance, think that playing foodless
makes the game easier (perhaps because I keep dying to acidic corpses).
I perform a foodless ascension thinking I am just using good strategy.
Have I completed a conduct?  I think so; the game wasn't any easier
because of my ignorance.  But it's a valid question.

That's all entirely separate from the question of whether the
protection racket makes the game harder.  And I have been undecided on
this for years, and will probably not come to a final decision for a
while yet, so don't go lumping me in either camp.


